We can read the same book and spend three hundred pages with the same character while picturing completely different faces. Then the movie comes out, and suddenly we have a face we can both point to. After a while, we might even forget the faces we made up.

And it runs the other way. Two people can look at the exact same picture and see different characters. One sees a king who stayed to defend his city. The other sees a man who stayed because he couldn’t bear to lose his throne. The image gives them something to point at together. They still get to disagree about who they’re pointing at.

That’s a lever for the Pantheon. Recast the king as a child, an old woman, or a machine. Keep the scene where the last boat leaves and the king decides to stay. Then change why the king stays and see which change makes the audience feel like they’re looking at someone else.